# Design direction

> Purpose: during **install**, if the dev did not give a design, the AI chooses the look and **writes the actual direction** into the project files. After install, those files are the design. Do not recommend again. Structure stays locked in `ui-styling.mdc`. The pack rules never name a palette.

## When it runs

| Moment | What the AI does |
|--------|------------------|
| **Install** | If the dev named no style, pattern, or palette: choose the Recommended direction and write it before install is complete |
| **After install** | Read `frontend-design/SKILL.md`, Profile → **Design direction**, and each route’s style lines. Build that. Do not open a new design recommendation |

Install is not complete while the skill still says `TBD` or “the AI will recommend.” The written files must name the style, tokens, type, icon, and the layout pattern for every route.

## Flow (`format.mdc`: Ask → Recommend → Decide → Write → Confirm)

This flow is **install only**.

1. **Ask** for constraints only: brand already decided (logo, brand color, fonts), light/dark preference, tone words, references. Dev-provided values **win**.
2. **Recommend** 2–3 directions as `REC-design-n` blocks, one **Recommended**. Each direction carries:
   | Part | Content |
   |------|---------|
   | Style | One name from the style list below, and why it fits **this** concept |
   | Palette | Primary, neutral ramp, surface/card/border, success/warn/danger — hex + role + measured contrast |
   | Mode | Light / dark / both (recommend both when users work long or at night) |
   | Type | UI/body + display pairing, weights, base size, line-height |
   | Shape & density | Radius, shadow/border style, spacing rhythm, compact vs airy |
   | Icons & motion | One icon key from `icons-states.mdc` (`material` · `fontawesome` · `flaticon` · `sf-symbols` · `lottie`). If the installer did not choose, recommend from the concept. Motion character |
   | Feel | One paragraph: what the main screen feels like |
3. **Decide** — the AI takes the **Recommended** direction and records it `AI-chosen`, in the same turn. Do not wait for the dev to name a style, a palette, or a font. A brand they already stated still wins. They may later say "warmer" or pick another option; then update the same files.
4. **Write** that decision into `frontend-design/SKILL.md` (style name, tokens, type, icons), the Tailwind theme, and Profile → **Design direction**. Status `DRAFT` until they approve it; the look is still in those files, not only in chat.

## Style (the AI picks one)

A style name is a spec to build from, not a mood word. The AI picks the style per page and per layout class. It does not ask the dev to name it. One view uses one style. Do not mix two styles on the same screen.

Each route in `route-layouts.mdc` records three lines — **Style — mobile**, **Style — tablet**, **Style — desktop** — each with a name from this list and the concrete rules for that class (type, surface, chrome). “Same as mobile” or “clean and modern” is not a style line. A landing may be **Bold campaign** while an admin list is **Utilitarian**. They still share the project tokens.

Every style below is a full spec. When a route names a style, follow **all** of its rows for that class. Write the chosen type, radius, and chrome into `frontend-design/SKILL.md` at install. Do not reduce a style to one sentence.

### Editorial
| | |
|--|--|
| Use | Reading, stories, magazines, long content |
| Type | Serif display, sans body, 16–18px body, wide measure (60–75ch), large headlines, small uppercase kicker |
| Surface | Paper off-white, ink near-black, one muted accent, hairline rules, no heavy shadow |
| Chrome | Quiet cards, text-first buttons, nav as a thin masthead, images full-bleed with a caption |
| Motion | Almost none. Section fade only. No bounce |
| Mobile | One column, large title, sticky thin title bar |
| Tablet | Title plus a narrow margin column for kickers |
| Desktop | Reading column plus a quiet aside (contents or pull quote) |
| Not | Card grids, bright fills, icon-only navigation |

